> Simple examples

Margins -1,0,.5,1,2 have hinge losses 2,1,.5,0,0. This exposes correctly classified points that still violate the unit margin.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/support_vector_machines_hinge.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto margin = n::tensor({-1, 0, .5, 1, 2}), loss = n::relu(1 - margin);
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.lines(margin.to_vector<double>(), loss.to_vector<double>(), "hinge loss")
      .title("Correct classification can still incur margin loss")
      .x_label("signed margin")
      .y_label("hinge loss");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(850, 420).save_svg("build/support_vector_machines_hinge.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  t.add_column("margin", margin.to_vector<double>());
  t.add_column("hinge", loss.to_vector<double>());
  std::print("{}\n", t.to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| margin | hinge |
| ---: | ---: |
| -1.0 | 2.0 |
| 0.0 | 1.0 |
| 0.5 | 0.5 |
| 1.0 | 0.0 |
| 2.0 | 0.0 |

\image(figures/support_vector_machines_hinge.svg)
